Content:
By Sebastian  Pellejero

Venezuela sits on the world’s largest oil reserves, yet pumps a meager fraction of them. Creditors may soon confront that same untapped potential. The country is preparing to restructure a towering debt load that stands at over twice its national output. With an economy in ruins, beset by weak institutions and the humanitarian crisis exacerbated by recent earthquakes, aggressive demands from lenders could create a self-defeating spiral.

Officials in Caracas, advised by boutique bank Centerview Partners, are expected to disclose liabilities close to $240 billion in the coming weeks, the Financial Times reported. Relative investor optimism has lifted government bond prices, if only to around 50 cents on the dollar for notes due next year. Under effective U.S. stewardship since the abduction of President Nicolas Maduro in January, a restructuring offers the prospect of at least some recoveries.

A crowded ledger of creditors complicate those hopes. Aside from bonds issued by the republic and state oil company PDVSA, a thicket of claims ranging from damages for forcibly nationalized assets through to oil-backed loans from China and Russia spread across jurisdictions.

Those numbers will almost certainly be in dispute. Venezuela has not presented credible national accounts in years. The International Monetary Fund cut ties in 2019 over a dispute about who represented the country, only resuming contact this past April.

More importantly, though, the productive capacity to repay debts is badly damaged. The nation’s GDP now sits around $118 billion, according to IMF data, down 68% from its 2012 peak. The economy rests on oil extraction, but current output of about 1 million barrels per day stands at a third the rate of its 1998 high. Each barrel now earns less for the government, too. Recent tax code changes lower levies and give operators more flexibility to spur oil investment.

Reviving production will be no easy feat. Holding output around current levels will cost some $52 billion through 2040, reckons consultancy Rystad Energy. Lifting production past 1.4 million daily barrels will take as much as $9 billion a year on top. Go beyond there, and the cost could rise even more steeply, requiring new infrastructure and scarce laborers to open new spigots. Such ambition would dwarf PDVSA's $10 billion budget contribution last year.

For interim President Delcy Rodriguez, who heads an uncertain government constrained by Washington, striking and sticking to any deal will be difficult. If creditors extract the most aggressive terms possible, then debt service costs could foreclose the investments needed to make repayments in the first place. There’s such a thing as too hard a bargain.

Follow Sebastian Pellejero on LinkedIn.

CONTEXT NEWS

Venezuela is set to reveal a $240 billion debt pile, significantly larger than earlier estimates, the Financial Times reported on June 24. Interim leader Delcy Rodriguez is aiming to reach a deal with creditors by the end of this year.
