Content:
By Robb M. Stewart

IsoEnergy has resumed exploration at its Larocque East uranium project in northern Saskatchewan following an evacuation of personnel due to the threat of nearby wildfires.

The Canadian company said Wednesday it is continuing with its summer drilling program at the project in the eastern Athabasca Basin after the temporary suspension.

Before work was halted late last month, four of a planned 20 holes were completed. IsoEnergy said each of those holes intersected mineralization.

Dan Brisbin, vice president of exploration, said early drilling on the project's Hurricane South Trend continue to reinforce the prospective nature of the trend and every completed hole has intersected anomalous radioactivity.

