Content:
Seres Therapeutics reported 80% of SER-155 recipients achieved an immunosuppressive-free clinical response at Day 15 in an IST for immune checkpoint inhibitor-related enterocolitis.

Key Highlights:

• 12 of 15 patients (80%) met primary endpoint: ≥1-grade diarrhea improvement without systemic immunosuppression at Day 15.
• 5 of 15 (33%) achieved immunosuppressive-free complete remission (Grade 0) at Day 15; 2 maintained remission at Day 43.
• SER-155 was generally well tolerated through Day 43 with no drug-related serious adverse events and no bloodstream infections reported.
• Pharmacology: robust bacterial strain engraftment and significant reductions in fecal calprotectin and fecal albumin by Day 43.
• Study supports continued development of SER-155 to treat irEC and potential expansion to other inflammatory and immune diseases.

Original SEC Filing: Seres Therapeutics, Inc. [ MCRB ] - 8-K - Jul. 08, 2026

