Content:
By Alexander Marrow

Coffee market volatility is preventing companies from lowering retail prices for consumers, Italian coffee roaster Lavazza said on Wednesday, describing instability as the new normal.

Coffee prices rose sharply in 2024 and reached ​record highs in 2025 as adverse weather hit ​supplies. Bean prices have generally trended lower this ⁠year, but surged on Monday as market participants repriced risks linked to an expected super El Nino climate event.

"We are living a long-lasting period of instability and uncertainty," Giuseppe Lavazza, chairman of the family-owned company, told reporters at Lavazza's flagship London store.

"For the time being it's too early to say 'the storm is over, the market is going to have stability, so it's time to think about a reduction of prices'."

The key to reducing volatility and removing the impact of speculation on the coffee market will be at least two years of good crops from top coffee-producing nations Brazil and Vietnam, Lavazza said.

"(We are) still not sure that the market is building the right stability that we are looking for," Lavazza said.

Cheaper prices for raw coffee beans take months to filter through to consumers, and continued volatility does not help.

Consumer demand has remained resilient even as prices have risen, Lavazza said, observing a trend for customers to drink less coffee rather than switch to a different brand.

"If prices go up, of course, you have to accept a little bit of reduction," Lavazza said. "Maybe you lose some consumers, but the core is still there."
