2026-07-08 12:51:00,241 [DEBUG] backend.langgraph_workflow: Built prompt: News Source: Reuters
Stock Symbol: 

Content:
The National Bank of Poland left its main interest rate at 3.75% on Wednesday, remaining cautious despite inflation slowing in June, as uncertainty in the Middle East strengthens expectations of faster price growth ahead, analysts said.

All 29 respondents in a Reuters poll published on Monday forecast the NBP would keep its main rate (PLINTR=ECI) unchanged in July.

"Today's MPC decision is not a surprise and, in fact, it could not have been otherwise. Inflation has begun to decline over the past two months, returning to the centre of the NBP's inflation target in June," said Monika Kurtek, chief economist at Bank Pocztowy.

"This has, in a sense, relieved the Council of the burden of considering interest rate increases."

Inflation unexpectedly slowed to 2.5% in June from 3.1% in May, more than analysts expected, bringing it squarely within the central bank's 1.5%-3.5% target range.

However, in the near future the pace of price growth may accelerate due to the expiry of a government programme limiting fuel price increases, while the situation in the Middle East remains uncertain and could affect commodity markets.

"This calls for extreme caution when it comes to easing monetary policy. In this situation, the Monetary Policy Council has no choice but to wait and observe the environment," Kurtek said.

"Rates were not changed today, and in the coming months, unless something very surprising happens, they will remain at their current levels – in my opinion, at least until the end of this year."

The market is now awaiting the Monetary Policy Council statement along with new central bank inflation projections due later on Wednesday. NBP Governor Adam Glapinski's press conference on Thursday should provide further insight into the Council's decision.

In July central bankers also review new NBP forecasts,  which should provide a clearer picture of inflation and economic growth in the coming quarters.

Poland's Monetary Policy Council kept its benchmark rate (PLINTR=ECI) at 3.75% for a third month in a row in June.

The governor of the National Bank of Poland, Adam Glapinski, said at the time of that decision that Polish interest rates were currently at an appropriate level, and slowing inflation had reduced the likelihood of monetary tightening.
